      "id": 1,
      "text": "Athmar Park was named by developer Raymond Erb in 1949‑1950 when he completed three subdivision plats called \"Athmar Park\".",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athmar_Park%2C_Denver"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood borders the South Platte River to the east, with a sharp rise in elevation near Pecos Street that climbs toward the southwest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athmar_Park%2C_Denver"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Aspgren Park honors Clifford Aspgren, a former Colorado House representative and long‑time resident, and is one of four Denver parks in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athmar_Park%2C_Denver"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Huston Lake Park sits in the center of Athmar Park and once housed Denver’s municipal animal shelter until 2011.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athmar_Park%2C_Denver"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Athmar branch library now hosts a Robert Mangold wind sculpture that originally stood at the Denver Art Museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athmar_Park%2C_Denver"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "South Platte River Trail, a popular five‑mile ride, starts in Athmar Park and loops to Confluence Park and downtown Denver.",
